  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nDry airmass in place across the region with a warm front well \nto the southwest from SE Nebraska to SW Missouri. This front is \ncurrently aiding scattered showers and storms across portions of\nIowa. While the front will make slow progress NE over the next \n24 hours, the area of precip will try to work NE well ahead of \nit, but run into increasingly dry air with some saturation \nstarting in the mid levels. Given the dry airmass, suspect more \nof a sprinkle vs light rain risk, but for now have just lowered \npops to slgt chc category. \n\nFocus then shifts to warmer temperatures on Saturday and\neventually some impacts from what will be left of Hurricane\nIsaias late in the weekend. Highs will climb into the 70s and\nmaybe a few 80s in far SW areas on Saturday prior to the arrival\nof a spoke of energy and inverted trough that pushes north\nSaturday night into early Sunday. The main sfc and upper level\nremnants will track along the Ohio River, reaching West Virginia\nby 6Z/12. An area of generally light rainfall will impact the \nsoutheast third to maybe half of the area. Bit higher rainfall\namounts are likely in far SE sections (SE of I-69) where some\nlocations could see up to a half inch. Wind gusts will pick up\nsomewhat as well in this window in the same areas. \n\nUpper level ridging will try to take hold into mid week, but a \nweak northern stream trough will drop in for Monday night into \nTuesday with another brief warmup Monday (maybe more locations \ntouching 80). Mid to late week will then feature likely phasing \nof northern and southern stream systems, carving out a deeper \ntrough with the sfc reflection remaining well NW of the area. A \nstrong cold front looks to bring a much more fall like air mass \nin its wake just outside the current period with 850 mb temps \ndropping below 0 C.",
